    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> went for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> as part of a summer offensive aimed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> oil fields, and the city became a matter of prestige for both Hitler and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a>.</li><li>After three months of house-to-house fighting in which the Germans took 90% of the ruined city, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> encircled the 6th Army from its flanks on 19 to 23 November 1942.</li><li>Casualty figures are widely disputed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> estimates from 800,000 to 1,500,000 and Soviet archival totals of over a million, and most German prisoners died in captivity.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Between 23 and 26 August 1942 Soviet reports record 955 people killed and 1,181 wounded in the bombing, while estimates of civilian dead from the bombing reach 40,000 or 70,000, though these may be exaggerated.</p></aside>
    </section>

