    <section class="battle-field" id="how-it-was-fought" data-battle="battle-of-stalingrad" data-v="e03387b889" aria-labelledby="battle-field-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="battle-field-title">How it was fought</h2>
      <p class="bf-lede">The field and the armies, phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back.</p>
      <ol class="bf-phases"><li><b>The 6th Army reaches Stalingrad</b> The 6th Army reached the outskirts of Stalingrad in pursuit of the Soviet 62nd and 64th Armies, which fell back into the city. The 4th Panzer Army had turned northwards to help take the city from the south. Romanian armies guarded the German flanks.</li><li><b>The Germans push the defenders to the river</b> The Germans advanced into the city at great cost and pushed the Soviet defenders back into narrow zones along the west bank of the Volga.</li><li><b>Operation Uranus overruns the Romanian 3rd Army</b> Three Soviet armies struck the northern flank of the 6th Army. The Romanian 3rd Army, with thin lines and outnumbered and poorly equipped, was overrun.</li><li><b>A second attack comes from the south</b> A second Soviet offensive of two armies was launched south of Stalingrad against the Romanian 4th Army Corps. The Romanian infantry were overrun by large numbers of tanks.</li><li><b>The ring closes at Kalach</b> The two Soviet forces raced west and met at Kalach, sealing the ring around Stalingrad. The 6th Army and the 4th Panzer Army were left inside it.</li><li><b>The relief force advances from Kotelnikovo</b> Operation Winter Storm was left with the LVII Panzer Corps around Kotelnikovo, supported by the rest of the 4th Panzer Army. By 18 December it had pushed to within 48 km of the 6th Army. The trapped army made no attempt to break out or link up.</li><li><b>The ring tightens and the 6th Army capitulates</b> Operation Koltso opened on 10 January 1943 with a very large bombardment, and the Germans retreated from the suburbs to the city. On 2 February the 6th Army capitulated, having exhausted its ammunition and food.</li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="background">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Background</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin.jpg" alt="Joseph Stalin" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StalinCropped1943.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Franklin D. Roosevelt Library Public Domain Photographs, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>By the spring of 1942 the German armed forces had captured vast territories of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> and the Baltic republics, but had not defeated it in a single campaign. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s fuel supplies had dwindled, and Hitler decided that the summer campaign of 1942 would aim at the southern Soviet Union and the oil fields of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> was one of the largest industrial centres of the Soviet Union and a transport hub on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. Controlling it would provide a supply route for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus-campaign">Caucasus campaign</a> and prevent Soviet shipments along the river. The city also bore the name of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Joseph Stalin</a>, the Soviet leader.</p><p>Stalin was convinced that the main German attack would fall on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. A Soviet offensive from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izium">Izyum</a> salient south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkov</a> began on 12 May and ended in a German encirclement that destroyed much of the Soviet force, leaving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> vulnerable to the summer offensive. Case Blue opened on 28 June 1942. Hitler divided the army group: Army Group A under Wilhelm List advanced towards the Caucasus, and Army Group B under Maximilian von Weichs, with Friedrich Paulus's 6th Army and Hermann Hoth's 4th Panzer Army, moved east towards the Volga and Stalingrad. On 23 July Hitler widened the objectives to include occupying the city. He is recorded as ordering that after its capture all male citizens be killed and women and children deported, because of their "thoroughly communistic" nature. By the end of July Soviet forces had been pushed back across the Don.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Stalingrad&#8221; cites where it covers Background, Prelude:</p><ul><li>David M. Glantz, Armageddon in Stalingrad: September-November 1942 (University Press of Kansas, 2009)</li><li>Wilhelm Adam, With Paulus at Stalingrad (Pen &amp; Sword Books, 2015)</li><li>Antony Beevor, Stalingrad (Viking, 1998)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="forces">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">The Two Sides</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> deployed five armies in and around the city (the 28th, 51st, 57th, 62nd and 64th). The 62nd Army under Vasiliy Chuikov was formed on 11 September and told to hold the city. When it fell back into the city on 12 September it had been reduced to 90 tanks, 700 mortars and just 20,000 personnel. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a>'s Order No. 227 of 27 July subjected commanders who ordered unauthorised retreats to military tribunal, and blocking detachments were posted behind Red Army units. At the start of the battle 75,000 women and girls from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> area who had finished military or medical training were to serve in it.</p><p>On the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> side the 6th Army and 4th Panzer Army of Army Group B attacked the city, supported by Luftflotte 4 under Wolfram von Richthofen, which began with 1,600 aircraft, of which 1,155 were operational. The Soviet air force was left with 192 serviceable aircraft by the end of August, 57 of them fighters. Italian, Hungarian and Romanian armies guarded the northern flank along the Don and the Romanian 4th Army the southern; the Hungarian 2nd Army held 200 km of front thinly. The 369th (Croatian) Reinforced Infantry Regiment was the only non-German unit selected to enter the city in the assault.</p><p>Chuikov's tactic was to "hug" the enemy, keeping Soviet positions as close as possible to German ones so that German aircraft and artillery could not strike without risking their own men. Soviet assault groups of 20 to 50 men moved through sewers and broke through walls.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="course">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How It Went</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>German forces first clashed with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> Front on 17 July in the bend of the Don. On 23 August the 6th Army and elements of the 4th Panzer Army launched their offensive, and Luftflotte 4 dropped some 1,000 tons of bombs; at least 90% of the city's housing stock was destroyed. The battle for the city itself began on 13 September. Mamayev Kurgan changed hands many times, and small groups held positions such as the grain elevator and Pavlov's House. By 27 September the Germans held the south of the city, the Soviets the centre and north and the ferries across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>.</p><p>After 27 September the fighting moved to the factory district. On 14 October, which Chuikov considered the worst day of the battle, a German assault reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tractor">tractor</a> factory and the Volga and split the 62nd Army in two; Hitler suspended all other operations on the Eastern Front. By November the Germans held 90% of the city, with Soviet forces in narrow pockets on the west bank. From 21 August to 20 November the 6th Army lost 60,548 men (12,782 killed, 45,545 wounded and 2,221 missing).</p><p>On 19 November the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> launched Operation Uranus, overrunning the Romanian 3rd Army on the 6th Army's northern flank; a second thrust on 20 November struck the Romanian 4th Army Corps to the south. The two met at Kalach on 23 November and sealed the ring. About 330,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> personnel were trapped, among them Germans, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, Italians and Croatians, and between 40,000 and 65,000 Hilfswillige ("Hiwis"), volunteer auxiliaries recruited from Soviet prisoners and civilians.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Stalingrad&#8221; cites where it covers Red Army, Initial attack, September city battles, Fighting in the industrial district:</p><ul><li>Antony Beevor, Stalingrad (Viking, 1998)</li><li>Wilhelm Adam, With Paulus at Stalingrad (Pen &amp; Sword Books, 2015)</li><li>David M. Glantz, Armageddon in Stalingrad: September-November 1942 (University Press of Kansas, 2009)</li><li>Chris Bellamy, Absolute War: Soviet Russia in the Second World War (Alfred A. Knopf &amp; Random House, 2007)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="aftermath">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Aftermath</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Hitler forbade a breakout and relied on an airlift, which delivered only 105 tons a day against the 750 tons needed. Operation Winter Storm, Manstein's relief attempt, came within 48 km of the 6th Army by 18 December and was abandoned on 23 December. On 7 January 1943 the Soviet command offered Paulus terms, including safety for prisoners, medical care and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/repatriation-of-cultural-property">repatriation</a> after the war; Paulus asked permission to surrender and Hitler refused. After Operation Koltso began on 10 January the airfields were lost, and on 30 January Hitler made Paulus a field marshal. The southern pocket collapsed on 31 January, and the northern one under Karl Strecker surrendered on 2 February.</p><p>Around 91,000 exhausted, ill, wounded and starving prisoners were taken, including 22 generals. Most died. Weakened by disease, starvation and lack of medical care during the encirclement, they were sent on forced marches to camps and later to labour camps across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. Some 35,000 were sent on transports, of which 17,000 did not survive. The last of the 5,000 to 6,000 survivors were repatriated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> in 1955.</p><p>Both sides disregarded civilians. German troops who reached a hospital for mentally handicapped children shot all the patients aged ten to fourteen, and Soviet machine-gun fire killed Russian women sheltering during the fighting at the Barrikady settlement. About 99% of the city was destroyed. After the battle a quick <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> found 1,515 people; Antony Beevor notes a census that counted 9,796 civilians, 994 of them children.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Stalingrad&#8221; cites where it covers Casualties, Luftwaffe losses, Aftermath:</p><ul><li>Victor Davis Hanson, The Second World Wars: How the First Global Conflict Was Fought and Won (Basic Books, 2020)</li><li>Antony Beevor, Stalingrad (Viking, 1998)</li><li>Omer Bartov, Hitler's Army: Soldiers, Nazis and War in the Third Reich (Oxford University Press, 1991)</li><li>Louis A. DiMarco, Concrete Hell: Urban Warfare from Stalingrad to Iraq (Osprey Publishing, 2012)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="sources">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">How We Know</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/georgy-zhukov"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/georgy-zhukov.jpg" alt="Georgy Zhukov" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/georgy-zhukov">Georgy Zhukov</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Георгий_Константинович_Жуков_в_военной_форме_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">не указан, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The battle is known from both armies' records and from what was written at the time. It is documented in letters and journals found on German dead, the memoirs of Chuikov and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgy-zhukov">Zhukov</a>, and the first-hand report of Alexander Werth, who visited the city on 3 to 5 February 1943. Omer Bartov studied 11,237 letters sent by soldiers inside the city between 20 December 1942 and 16 January 1943, and Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkvd">NKVD</a> and Don Front reports survive.</p><p>The losses are disputed. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a>, estimates range from 800,000 to 1,500,000 casualties (killed, wounded or captured): Peter H. Wilson gives 800,000 including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a>, and the multivolume Great Patriotic War history and Geoffrey Jukes give up to 1.5 million, which has been called an overestimate. For the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, archival figures give 1,129,619 casualties (478,741 killed or missing and 650,878 wounded or sick); a later revision gives 1,347,214. Boris Sokolov puts the dead at over two million, but many Russian historians regard his figures as unreliable.</p><p>Civilian losses are also uncertain. Soviet reports give 955 killed and 1,181 wounded between 23 and 26 August, while estimates of civilians killed by the bombing reach 40,000 or 70,000, which may be exaggerated. Incomplete party archives record 42,754 residents dead; Tatyana Pavlova calculates 185,232 dead of 710,000 inhabitants, or 235,232 with the rural areas. Historians also disagree over Soviet coercion. Beevor claims 13,500 Soviet soldiers were executed, which has been disputed; Richard Overy warns against reducing the Soviet effort to coercion.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/george-vi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-vi.jpg" alt="George VI" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_George_VI_LOC_matpc.14736_A_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matson Photo Service, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a> ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>'s 1942 summer offensive and passed the strategic initiative on the Eastern Front to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>. It is generally regarded as the turning point of the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>; a 1989 quantitative study and a 2004 analysis reached the same conclusion, and Hitler himself blamed Stalingrad for Germany's impending doom in a speech on 9 November 1944. The German public was told of the defeat at the end of January 1943, and on 18 February <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels">Joseph Goebbels</a> gave his Sportpalast speech calling for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/total-war">total war</a>.</p><p>In the Soviet Union confidence in victory rose, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-stalin">Stalin</a> was made a Marshal of the Soviet Union. The news was said to have inspired resistance movements in occupied Europe. A ceremonial Sword of Stalingrad was forged for King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a> and given to Stalin by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winston-churchill">Winston Churchill</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> Conference. Stalingrad was awarded the title Hero City in 1945, and The Motherland Calls was erected on Mamayev Kurgan in 1967. In post-Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> the victory is among the Days of Military Honour; Volgograd held a parade on 2 February 2013, and parades have been held there since. Every year hundreds of bodies of soldiers are recovered and reburied in cemeteries at Mamayev Kurgan or Rossoshka.</p><p>Geoffrey Roberts calls it the biggest and most traumatic defeat in German military history, and Glantz and others describe it as the most brutal clash of the war. Joe Castillo argues that Stalin's leadership and Order No. 227 turned the battle into a deliberate bloodbath for Soviet troops.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Battle of Stalingrad&#8221; cites where it covers Significance:</p><ul><li>P. M. H. Bell, Twelve Turning Points of the Second World War (Yale University Press, 2011)</li><li>Roberts Geoffrey, Victory at Stalingrad (Routledge, 2002)</li><li>Geoffrey Roberts, Stalin's Wars: From World War to Cold War, 1939–1953 (Yale University Press, 2006)</li></ul></details>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volgograd">Stalingrad</a>, from the summer of 1942 to 2 February 1943, German and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> forces fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> for a ruined city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> until the Soviet counteroffensive encircled the 6th Army. Casualty figures are uncertain, civilians and prisoners died in great numbers, and the battle is generally seen as the war's turning point in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</em></p>

